# PART 11 — BENCHMARK METRICS & SCORING

Use task-specific evaluation profiles.

Do not force one universal score on all workloads.

## Required metrics

### Quality

- Exact accuracy.
- Semantic correctness.
- Precision.
- Recall.
- F1.
- Reasoning score.
- Instruction compliance.
- Citation accuracy.
- Groundedness.

### Errors

Separate:

- Logic errors.
- Factual errors.
- Hallucinations.
- Formatting errors.
- Schema errors.
- Tool-selection errors.
- Tool-execution errors.
- Timeout errors.
- Provider errors.
- Safety violations.

### Performance

- Average latency.
- Median latency.
- P50.
- P90.
- P95.
- Time to first token.
- Throughput.
- Tokens per second where measurable.

### Cost

- Input tokens.
- Output tokens.
- Cost per request.
- Cost per successful task.
- Estimated cost per 1,000 tasks.
- Retry overhead.
- Fallback overhead.

### Reliability

- Request success rate.
- Provider availability.
- Retry success.
- Timeout rate.
- Fallback frequency.
- Invalid output percentage.

### Statistical reporting

For each result report:

- Number of test cases.
- Number of runs.
- Mean.
- Median.
- Standard deviation.
- Confidence interval.
- Evaluation date.
- Dataset version.

If a category has insufficient samples, label its ranking LOW CONFIDENCE or INSUFFICIENT DATA.

## Overall score

Keep the existing scoring weights as a versioned initial scoring profile, not a universal truth.

Allow separate scoring profiles for coding, reasoning, RAG, Urdu, business analysis and agent execution.

Never hide component scores behind one attractive total.

